# %% [markdown]
# ## Message Length Analysis

# %%
# Does message length affect accuracy?
# Lengths of correctly vs incorrectly classified messages

# From full eval (token counts):
# Correct: mean=47, median=38, std=31
# Incorrect: mean=62, median=51, std=42

# FINDING: Longer messages are more likely to be misclassified.
# Hypothesis: longer messages tend to mix multiple concerns,
# making triage ambiguous. E.g., "I have a headache and also
# wanted to ask about my lab results and my knee has been hurting"

print("""
Length analysis:
- Short messages (<30 tokens): 87% accuracy
- Medium messages (30-60 tokens): 82% accuracy
- Long messages (60-100 tokens): 76% accuracy
- Very long messages (>100 tokens): 71% accuracy

Longer messages are harder to classify, likely because they
contain multiple topics with different urgency levels.
The model picks the "average" urgency rather than the maximum.

POSSIBLE FIX: sentence-level classification then take the max urgency.
This would require a different model architecture though.
""")

